If both epsilon values are $1$, choose the boundary representatives of step 2.1. Both horizontal parts are cycles; if $x_h=d_ha$, then $(x\otimes y)_h=d_h(a\otimes y_h)$. Its vertical part is the distinguished tensor class, so the converse in step 2.1 gives $F_{s+t}=0$, and epsilon of the tensor is $1$. The same proof works when one factor has epsilon zero and the other has epsilon $1$, using a cycle representative from step 3.1 for the zero factor. If both factors have epsilon zero, tensor their southwest-hook lifts to obtain $G_{s+t}
e0$. Their duals also have epsilon zero by step 2.2; applying the same lift construction to the dual tensor gives its $G_{-(s+t)}
e0$, hence $F_{s+t}
e0$ on the original tensor. Its epsilon is therefore zero. Dualizing the already proved positive cases proves the negative/negative and zero/negative cases. Together with [F3], these are exactly both tensor rules in the Statement, with actual representatives and compatible homotopies.

